From 234b025fbe7f1c40fe6c76d1c316c5c66c40898f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian <christian@find.xyz>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 10:44:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hf client: route SCO over HCI; btm: bridge voice between two
 SCO links

The hands-free client hardcoded offload=true in all seven
esco_parameters_for_codec() calls, so the phone's voice link was set up
with data path = PCM and the audio went to the controller's unwired PCM
pins. Follow hfp_hal_interface::get_offload_enabled() like the AG does
(data path = HCI when bluetooth.hfp.software_datapath.enabled is set).

New stack/btm/btm_sco_bridge.{h,cc}: when bluetooth.neo.sco_bridge.enabled
is set (sampled at each SCO connect), every in-band SCO packet is forwarded
verbatim to the other connected in-band SCO link through a byte ring per
link, re-packetised to the destination's packet length. Same codec required
on both links; a mismatch is logged once and dropped. Hooked into
btm_route_sco_data() before the single-active-SCO check (which would have
dropped the second link's packets), btm_sco_connected() and the disconnect
handler. Adds btm_send_sco_packet_to(handle, data).

This is what lets a phone call reach the earbuds through the dash: the
dash is HFP client to the phone and HFP AG to the earbuds, and the Android
audio framework cannot represent two SCO links.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
